---
title: Irish Stew (Instant Pot Slow Cook)
description: A gentle, brothy slow-cooked version using the requested rump steak and potatoes.
source: Original blend — see the source-blend note at the end of the file
servings: 6
time required: 6 hours 30 minutes
prep time: 20 minutes
cook time: 6 hours
course: main
cuisine: Irish
diet: nut-free
tags: stew, irish, instant pot, slow cook, beef, comfort food
---

Season @rump steak{800%g}(trimmed, cut into ~4 cm chunks and patted dry)
with @sea salt{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}.

Set the #Instant Pot{} to Sauté. Add @olive oil{2%tbsp} and brown the beef
in batches for ~{6%minutes} total. Transfer it to a #plate{}.

Add @onion{2}(quartered and sliced) to the pot and sauté for ~{4%minutes},
then add @garlic{3%cloves}(crushed) and cook for ~{1%minute}. Pour in some
of the @beef stock{500%ml} and scrape up the browned bits from the base.

Return the beef and its juices to the pot. Add the remaining stock,
@thyme{4%sprigs}, @bay leaves{2}, @potatoes{750%g}(peeled and cut into
~3 cm chunks), and @carrots{3}(peeled and cut into ~2 cm pieces). Stir to
combine.

Cancel Sauté. Select Slow Cook on the low setting (called Less on many
Instant Pot models). Use a glass lid if you have one; if using the pressure
cooking lid, set the steam release to Venting. Cook for ~{6%hours}, keeping
the lid closed. Instant Pot setting names vary by model, so check your
manual for the low setting.

Check that the rump is tender and the vegetables are cooked through. Rump
is lean, so begin checking around ~{5%hours}: if the vegetables are ready
before the meat, lift them out and keep warm while the meat continues.
Continue cooking in ~{30%minute} increments if the beef is still firm.

Mash a few potato chunks against the side of the pot and stir them in to
thicken the broth. Taste and adjust @sea salt{} and @black pepper{} as
needed. Stir through @flat leaf parsley{1/4%cup}(roughly chopped), saving
some to scatter on top.

Serve in deep bowls with bread for dunking.

> Swaps to consider: replace some of the stock with @?Guinness{250%ml} for a stout version, or add @?Worcestershire sauce{1%tbsp} for savoury depth. For the traditional lamb version, use @?lamb shoulder{800%g}; it may need more time, so check tenderness before serving.

> Source blend — this is an original adaptation, not a transcription. The AP's Irish lamb stew informed the potato-and-onion base and crushed-potato thickener. Food Network's rump-roast guidance informed the low-and-slow approach and its 7–8 hour low timing for a whole roast; this recipe uses a shorter, untested estimate for steak-sized chunks. Instant Pot's official guidance informed the Slow Cook setting and lid/venting instructions; settings vary by model. https://apnews.com/article/saint-patricks-recipe-irish-lamb-stew-be4b39bfc1f7a49a81c7b520b67a405b https://www.foodnetwork.com/how-to/packages/food-network-essentials/rump-roast https://instantpot.com.ph/smart-cooking-programs
